- 2026-10-21 Tauri CSP hardening vs. the srcdoc preview. `tauri.conf.json` sets `dangerousDisableAssetCspModification` to `["script-src"]`. `dist/index.html` carries one build-injected inline script (the gateway token), so the default asset hardening adds a nonce-source to `script-src` and, per CSP3, any nonce/hash in a source list voids `'unsafe-inline'`. The srcdoc preview inherits the host policy but cannot carry a compile-time nonce, so in packaged builds every inline script the preview injects was rejected (html-to-image capture, preview bridge, feedback engine): the comment layer stayed inert and PNG export timed out after 15s with `failed`. Browser dev was unaffected because it runs without the Tauri policy. The `style-src` hardening is retained — `dist` has no inline style, so `'unsafe-inline'` there still applies and preview styling was never affected.

- 2026-10-21 Comment layer inert: ASI across concatenated bridge scripts. `buildSrcdoc` inlines `PREVIEW_CORE_JS + '\n' + FEEDBACK_JS` into one `<script>`; `PREVIEW_CORE_JS` closed with `})()` and no semicolon while `FEEDBACK_JS` opened with `(function(){`, so the parser read them as a call chain `(intermediate value)(...)()`. The preview bridge ran, the feedback engine was passed as an argument and never evaluated — PNG capture and height reporting worked, but no hover highlight, no crosshair cursor and no comment editor existed, while the host still set `fbActive` unconditionally after its one-way postMessage. Fix: `PREVIEW_CORE_JS` closes with `})();`, `FEEDBACK_JS` opens with `;(function(){`, and the concatenation site inserts `'\n;\n'`, so neither snippet depends on how a caller joins them.

- 2026-10-22 Installer handoff fixes. `vite.config.ts` `gatewayTokenPlugin` gained `apply: 'serve'`: without it the plugin also ran during `vite build` and baked the build machine's `~/.pebble/gateway.token` into `dist/index.html` and the installer, so every other machine's handshake closed 4401. `gatewayClient.ts` now resolves the token through the Tauri `get_gateway_token` command first and only falls back to `window.__PEBBLE_GATEWAY_TOKEN__` in a plain browser, and `_tokenLoaded` latches only after a token is actually obtained (previously an early null from the still-starting sidecar made every reconnect send no token). `DEFAULT_PROJECT_PATH` is now empty instead of `E:\workspace`; `_read_projects()` returns `{"active":"","projects":[]}` instead of seeding the registry with the sidecar's working directory (which could be the install directory); `config.ts addProject` returns `{ok,error,path}` instead of swallowing failures. New `ProjectSetupDialog.vue` guides the first launch (opens once per run when the gateway connects with no project path, reuses the cfm-* dialog shell), `InputArea` intercepts send while no project is chosen and dispatches `pebble:request-project-setup`. Verified: vue-tsc clean, vite build clean, `dist` contains no token value and no `E:\workspace`.

- 2026-10-22 Mobile LAN access. A second, opt-in TLS listener (`wss://0.0.0.0:19877`) now serves mobile clients while the desktop app keeps using plain `ws://127.0.0.1:19876`; loopback never leaves the machine, so the desktop path stays untouched and needs no certificate trust. `gateway/lan_access.py` owns a self-signed RSA-2048 certificate (10-year, SAN = localhost + every non-loopback IPv4, `{PEBBLE_HOME}/lan/cert.pem` + `key.pem` at 0600), its SHA-256 fingerprint (colon-separated uppercase, the shape Android displays for pinning), LAN IP discovery via a UDP connect that sends no packet, and single-use pairing codes (`secrets.token_urlsafe(24)`, 5-minute TTL, one live code at a time, unknown-code failures rate-limited to 10 per 60 s). The QR payload deliberately carries a pairing code rather than the gateway token: `handle_ws` accepts `?pair=` only when `_is_tls()` is true, replies with a `gateway.paired` event holding the token over the encrypted channel, then closes 1000 so the client reconnects with the token, so a photographed screen leaks nothing durable. `gateway/rpc/pairing.py` adds status / session / enable / disable / revoke / rotate_certificate, with enable and disable hot-applying through a `register_apply_hook` indirection (a direct import would cycle ws_server -> rpc -> ws_server); `_apply_lan_config` uses `loop.create_task` when it is already on the event loop, because `run_coroutine_threadsafe(...).result()` from the loop thread deadlocks. `cryptography` is optional: absent, `pairing.status` reports `supported=false` plus an install hint and loopback keeps working. Verified: ruff clean, syntax compiled, pairing lifecycle (single-use, replay rejected, new code invalidates the old, lockout after 10 failures) exercised against the live module; the TLS handshake itself is untested until `cryptography` is installed.
- 2026-10-22 Desktop pairing UI, and the QR renderer turned out to be broken. `render_qr_svg` wrote segno's SVG output into an `io.StringIO`, but segno's serializer emits bytes, so every call raised `TypeError: string argument expected, got 'bytes'` and the helper swallowed it into `None`; the panel would have silently shown the URI fallback with no QR at all. Fixed by using `BytesIO` and decoding. `pairing.session` now also returns `uri` and `qr_svg` (previously it stopped at code/expires_in, so the desktop had nothing to render), and rejects the call when no LAN address is detected instead of emitting a `pebble://pair` URI with a null host. New `PairingPanel.vue` hangs off the TitleBar next to the pricing button: `pairing.status` on open, one-time code + inline SVG QR, enable/disable toggle, copy buttons for address/code/fingerprint, a local countdown that drops an expired code, and a footer stating the token is exchanged over TLS and never displayed. The QR is only rendered through `v-html` after an `<svg` prefix check; anything else falls back to the copyable URI. Because `pairing.enable` is applied on the gateway's own event loop, the panel waits 400 ms before re-reading status, otherwise a failed bind would look like a healthy "enabled" state. Verified end to end against a real TLS listener: handshake completes, the peer certificate's SHA-256 matches `certificate_fingerprint()` byte for byte, a valid code over TLS yields `gateway.paired` then close 1000, replaying that code gets 4401, the same code over plain ws gets 4403, and the plain-text attempt leaves the code still redeemable (an attacker cannot burn a legitimate code without TLS). `vue-tsc --noEmit` clean, `vite build` clean in 11.7 s, and the rebuilt dist still contains neither `yh21GLNO` nor `Z2jU0lSA` nor `E:\workspace` (the only `__PEBBLE_GATEWAY_TOKEN__` occurrence is the expected browser-fallback read).

## Android client

- Native companion app under `android-client/` (Kotlin + Jetpack Compose, single `:app` module, no XML layouts, no ViewModel layer)
- Transport: hand-rolled JSON-RPC 2.0 over an OkHttp WebSocket with certificate-fingerprint pinning and a single-use pairing code; payloads read via `org.json` with explicit defaults. `GatewayClient` exposes `ConnState` (`Idle`/`Pairing`/`Connecting`/`Connected`/`Failed`) and a `SharedFlow<GatewayEvent>` for server pushes
- Event routing is app-scoped in `GlobalChatStore` so a running stream survives tab switches. `permission.request` is routed by its `session_id` into `pendingApprovals` (deduplicated by `request_id`); `clarify.request` carries no session id and is attributed to `activeSessionId` into `pendingClarifies`; all remaining events go to `activeSessionId`'s `ConversationState`
- Conversation: streamed `message.delta` / `thinking.delta` accumulation, tool cards driven by `tool.start` / `tool.complete`, `message.complete` reconciles the transcript to the authoritative final text, `session.interrupt` stops a run, `session.resume` loads history
- Approval sheet is rendered by `AppShell` above every route, head of queue by default and pinned to a specific request when opened from the Inbox; decisions post `permission.decide` and move to `resolved`
- Screens: Sessions (`session.list`), Conversation, Changes (`session.file_changes` / `session.file_diff` / `session.file_accept`), Usage (`usage.stats`), Inbox (aggregated approvals + clarifications with an "approve all" that skips high risk), Settings (connection, project/agent/model read back from the gateway, appearance, behaviour)
- Accent theming is dynamic: `Prefs.accentKey` selects one of five `AccentTone`s, provided through `LocalAccentTone` and read at call sites via `Palette.primary`/`accent`/`border`; `PebbleColors` holds only fixed neutrals and status colours. Behaviour toggles persist in the same `Prefs` (SharedPreferences)
- Fenced code is tokenized by `ui/common/CodeHighlight.kt`; blocks clip at 8000 characters before highlighting because tokenization happens on the UI thread inside `remember`
- `:app:assembleDebug` is the verification gate; the APK builds clean

## Mobile parity pass

- Approval offers three remember chips (15 min / 1 hour / session). `remember_minutes = -1` stores a no-expiry trust entry keyed by session + permission type; `0` asks again; `0` is also what tapping the active chip restores
- `model.options` returns `pricing` and `currency` alongside providers, read from `model_pricing.json` in the data dir, so the model picker shows `¥x in / ¥y out per 1M` without a second round trip
- Sessions list groups into Today / Yesterday / date, badges sessions holding a pending approval, tints the session that owns the live stream, and shows a socket indicator in its header

## Multi-device event flow

- `session.resume` returns message rows whose identifier field is `message_id` (not `id`); the Android client reads `message_id` first and folds the row index into the render key so a duplicate or missing id can never collide in a lazy list
- `ws_server.WebSocketWriter.write_event` broadcasts to every live connection rather than only the requester, and injects `session_id` into the payload. Stream deltas carry no session of their own, so both clients need that field to tell whose run they are looking at
- The Vue store ignores stream frames whose `session_id` differs from the session on screen; non-stream events (`session.updated`, `file.change`, `todos.update`, clarify, permission) are handled regardless of origin
- A completed pairing is stored in `Prefs` (host, port, fingerprint, code, token) and restored on launch, so the app reconnects instead of re-prompting; disconnecting clears it

## Cross-client state sync

- `server.set_event_broadcaster` / `broadcast_event` is the fan-out hook the WebSocket
  server registers at startup; RPC handlers call it when state changes that every client
  must see
- `permission.decide` broadcasts `permission.resolved` and `clarify.respond` broadcasts
  `clarify.resolved`; both carry `request_id`, so a prompt answered on one client
  disappears on the other
- Clients react: Android drops the matching `pendingApprovals` / `pendingClarifies` entry and
  clears the in-conversation card; the Vue store marks the inline question answered and
  dispatches `gateway:permission-resolved`
- QR scanning: the one-shot latch only trips on payloads starting with `pebble://`, so a
  stray QR cannot deaden the scanner; analysis resolution is 1280x720 and `ALSO_INVERTED` is
  set so a code rendered on a dark panel still decodes

## Model, agent and billing sync

- `model.switch` and `model.provider.set` both write the same agent model config, so the two
  clients share one source of truth; `agent.switch` sets the process-wide current agent.
  Neither used to notify anyone, so an open screen kept showing the old value
- Both now broadcast `model.changed` / `agent.changed`; Android mirrors them into
  `GlobalChatStore.currentModel` / `currentAgent`, which the conversation header and the
  Settings workspace card read instead of each fetching once on open
- `message.usage` was only consumed by the desktop. Android now counts those frames and
  re-queries `usage.stats` for the session range, so the Usage tab tracks a running turn
  (the aggregate still comes from the backend, not from summed deltas)

## Cross-client interaction hardening

- Android routes every gateway frame by the `session_id` it carries instead of the active screen,
  so a background run keeps its transcript; settled approval/clarify frames fan out to all sessions
- `ConversationState` loads once per session (`ensureLoaded`) and resets its stream anchors on
  load, so re-entering a conversation no longer appends deltas into a history message
- `session.resume` reports `running`, letting the client restore the composer from gateway state
  rather than from event traffic a dropped socket would have eaten
- New `permission.list` / `clarify.list`; `AppShell` calls `syncPending` on every connect so a
  reconnecting client rebuilds its inbox from gateway memory
- `PebbleRoot` runs a capped-backoff reconnect loop; `GatewayClient` funnels frames through an
  unbounded channel instead of losing them when the shared-flow buffer fills
- Approval session ids are thread-local in `PermissionManager`, so two concurrent turns no longer
  relabel each other's requests
- `session.file_accept` / `session.file_revert` broadcast `files.accepted` / `files.reverted`, and
  both clients refresh their change list from them
- Desktop: a `clarify.request` belonging to another session is held per session instead of
  hijacking the open transcript; `markQuestionState` (called twice but never defined) is
  implemented, fixing a ReferenceError that aborted `clarify.resolved` handling

## Cross-client message and permission parity

- `prompt.submit` now broadcasts `user_message.saved` with `text` and `created_at`, not just the
  id: the row that carries the text only exists on the client that sent it, so the other one had
  nothing to render and showed the reply without the question
- Desktop creates the streaming container on the first `message.delta` / `thinking.delta` /
  `tool.start` when no run is open locally (`agentMsgIdRef` empty), so a reply produced by the
  phone appears live instead of only after a reload
- Desktop `PermissionDialog` subscribes to `gateway:permission-resolved`; a decision taken on the
  phone now closes the sheet instead of leaving it asking about a settled request
- Android `ConversationState` handles `user_message.saved`, appending the message unless the
  trailing user row already carries the same text

## Mobile localisation and text scale

- `ui/i18n/Strings.kt` adds `L` (English-keyed dictionary, ~180 entries) and `FontScale`; Settings
  gains Language and Text size cards, both persisted in `Prefs` and restored before the first frame
- Every screen in the Android client now renders through `L.t(...)`, including reminder strings
  built with templates (date separators, session message counts, risk tags, change counters)
- Protocol values stay untranslated: `UsageScreen` range keys and `ApprovalSheet` remember minutes
  keep their raw values, with labels built per composition instead of in a top-level val

## Transcript restore, session fan-out and notifications

- Android decoded stored messages with the wrong keys (`content`/`tool_name`/`tool_context`);
  `session.resume` actually returns `text`/`name`/`context`, so a reopened conversation rendered
  as empty bubbles and tool cards titled "tool". `ChatMessage.from` now accepts both spellings and
  the gateway includes a tool row's output as `text`
- `session.create` broadcasts `session.created`; the desktop adds the row to its sidebar (without
  stealing focus) and Android refreshes its list instead of waiting for the 4s poll
- `data/Notifier.kt` posts system notifications for replies, permission requests and questions when
  the app is backgrounded; `AppVisibility` tracks foreground state via activity callbacks, the
  channel owns sound and vibration, and Settings gained a Notifications toggle that requests
  `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` on API 33+

## Background delivery

- Root cause of both reported symptoms was the same: a backgrounded Android process is frozen, so
  WebSocket frames never reach the app — no notification and no transcript entry. `ConnectionService`
  (foreground, `dataSync`) now keeps the process running while paired
- `AppResume.tick` drives a foreground resync: `AppShell` re-pulls pending approvals/questions and
  `ConversationScreen` reloads the transcript, guarded by `session.resume`'s `running` flag so a
  live stream is never clobbered
- `AppVisibility` now tracks resumed/paused instead of started/stopped

## Notification dispatch, foreground reload and camera binding

- Notifications moved from `GlobalChatStore` into `GatewayClient.onMessage` via `Notifier.dispatch`:
  a backgrounded app can lose its composition while the process lives, so tying an alert to "a
  screen is mounted" lost the notification entirely
- `ConversationState.refresh` now waits up to 12s for a `Connected` state before reloading. The
  old code fired immediately on resume, failed with "not connected", and left the previous rows and
  the send-error banner on screen — the state that only a tab switch appeared to clear
- A failed send now reports "Connection lost" when the socket is down instead of "Send failed"
- QR scanner: `LocalContext` is unwrapped before the `LifecycleOwner` cast (a wrapper made it null
  and rendered nothing), the deprecated `setTargetResolution` is gone (it refuses to bind on
  sensors without that size, leaving a black box), and a binding exception is now shown on screen
  instead of being swallowed

## Notification icon, service start and QR decoding

- Notification and foreground-service icons now use an app-owned drawable. The platform drawables
  (`stat_sys_data_bluetooth`, `stat_notify_chat`) are not present on every OEM build, and a missing
  one makes `startForeground` throw, killing the service with no trace and blocking every alert
- `ConnectionService.start` moved from a Compose effect to `MainActivity.onCreate`, guarded by the
  stored pairing; `running` and `lastError` are exposed so Settings can report whether it came up
- Settings gains a `Send test notification` action that bypasses the enable flag and the foreground
  check, separating "notifications are broken" from "no event fired"
- `QrDecoder` converts frames with `ImageProxy.toBitmap()` and `RGBLuminanceSource` instead of
  hand-assembling the YUV planes; a mis-assembled buffer still looks like a valid bitmap and simply
  never decodes
- A dropped socket now fails every in-flight request immediately instead of leaving each to time out

## Making the two silent failures visible

- `ConnectionService.status()` now distinguishes four outcomes: `running`, `start rejected: …`
  (startForegroundService threw), `failed: …` (startForeground threw, caught as Throwable because
  the failure type varies by Android version), and `not started` (onStartCommand never ran). The
  previous version reported an empty reason for the last case, which was indistinguishable from a
  dead button
- `Notifier.testNow` returns its outcome instead of Unit and Settings shows it; a missing
  POST_NOTIFICATIONS grant now also triggers the system permission prompt from the same tap
- Service start is unconditional in `onCreate`; it no longer depends on a stored pairing

## Service start diagnostics

- `ConnectionState` (Compose state, in `data/Notifier.kt`) now holds `running`, `lastError`,
  `launchError` and `lastLaunchAt`; the service writes it and Settings reads it, so the row updates
  when the service comes up after the screen has already composed (the previous plain `@Volatile`
  left a stale "not started" on screen)
- `lastLaunchAt` separates "never asked" from "asked and the system ignored it", which is the
  difference between a missing call and an OEM policy refusing the service
- `ConnectionService.start` is retried from `Activity.onResume`: at `onCreate` the activity is not
  yet visible, and some Android builds refuse a foreground service from that state

## One-pass background and pairing hardening

- QR: `QrDecoder` now decodes through `RGBLuminanceSource` from both a camera frame
  (`ImageProxy.toBitmap()`) and a picked bitmap; PairingScreen gained a `Choose from gallery`
  action that decodes a screenshot of the desktop panel, bypassing the camera entirely
- Background: `onTaskRemoved` re-arms the service through `AlarmManager` (a direct start there is
  rejected as a background start on API 31+); `REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS` and
  `WAKE_LOCK` declared; Settings gained `Allow background running` (battery whitelist) and
  `Auto-start settings` (probes the known OEM auto-start activities, falls back to app details)
- `ConnectionState.describe()` distinguishes running / start rejected / failed / started but not
  running / never started

## Keeping the socket alive in the background

- Root cause of the disappearing notifications: a foreground service raises priority but Android
  still suspends the process. Once suspended, OkHttp stops sending its 20s pings, the gateway's
  `ping_timeout=10` (with `ping_interval=30`) drops the link, and every later event is lost until
  the app is opened. A `PARTIAL_WAKE_LOCK` held by `ConnectionService` is what actually keeps the
  socket alive
- The reconnect loop moved from `PebbleRoot`'s `LaunchedEffect` into `GatewayClient.startAutoReconnect`
  (its own process-scoped coroutine): a loop tied to the composition stops retrying exactly when a
  backgrounded app loses it
- QR gallery decoding now tries several passes — as-is, downscaled, centre crop at two scales, four
  corner crops, then the original resolution — because a screenshot puts the code in an arbitrary
  part of a much larger image

## Pairing QR was physically too small to decode

- Root cause found by measuring instead of guessing: the 114-character payload produced a
  **version 7** code (45x45 modules), rendered with `border=2` and capped at 190px by the panel
  CSS, which works out to **3.9 pixels per module**. A phone camera cannot resolve that, so both
  the live scan and a screenshot failed — the crop strategy added earlier was addressing a
  problem that did not exist
- Fix is fourfold: the certificate digest in the URI is truncated to 128 bits (82-char payload,
  version 5), error correction dropped to L, the quiet zone restored to the spec's 4 modules, and
  the panel width raised to 240px. Net result **5.33 px/module**, a 37% increase
- `PairingUri` accepts a 32- or 64-hex digest and `GatewayClient` compares it as a prefix of the
  certificate's SHA-256, so older full-length links still pair

## QR decoder rewritten stateless; wakelock surfaced

- `QrDecoder` no longer keeps a shared `MultiFormatReader`: ZXing's reader holds per-decode
  state and is not thread-safe, so a second call could see a half-reset reader that finds
  nothing. Each attempt now builds its own and runs three independent passes — plain
  `QRCodeReader` with no hints, `MultiFormatReader` + TRY_HARDER, then an explicitly inverted
  image — over the same ARGB pixels
- `ConnectionState.wakeLockHeld` is reported alongside `running`, so Settings can show whether the
  partial wake lock was actually acquired
- `test-qr.png` (450x450, version 5, quiet zone 4) and `test-qr-desktop-params.png` (360x360, the
  exact parameters the panel renders) are generated from the same payload for a decisive test:
  if the app cannot decode the clean one either, the fault is the decoder, not the panel

## Pairing code legible at camera distance; Wi-Fi kept awake

- Confirmed the decoder works: the generated `test-qr.png` scans and attempts to connect, so the
  earlier failure was the on-screen code, not ZXing
- The panel renders the code at 240px, which leaves a phone camera one or two sensor pixels per
  module. Two changes: `shape-rendering: crispEdges` so module edges are not anti-aliased, and
  clicking the code opens a full-screen view at `min(80vmin, 600px)`
- Background: added a `WIFI_MODE_FULL_HIGH_PERF` Wi-Fi lock next to the partial wake lock — Wi-Fi
  power save throttles packets once the screen is off, which alone misses the ping window. The
  gateway's `ping_timeout` also went from 10s to 60s so a delayed pong no longer drops the socket

## Interleaved reasoning was flattened on Android

- Three separate defects produced the reported shape. Streaming: `startTool` did not reset
  `thinkingIdx` / `assistantIdx`, so every later `thinking.delta` kept appending to the first
  card. History: `toItem` rendered only `content` and dropped `reasoning`, so every trace card
  vanished on reload
- The third is the interesting one: the gateway stores the whole interleaved sequence
  (reasoning / text / tool_execution) in the `tool_calls` column, and the desktop rebuilds the
  order from it. Android treated that column as tool output, which collapsed a multi-step turn
  into one block and pushed all traces to the end. `toItems` now parses the same field and emits
  one item per element, with fallbacks when a record lacks text or a trace
- `completeMessage` no longer overwrites a block that already streamed: its `text` is the whole
  turn, so writing it into the last block duplicated every earlier segment

## Background reconnect no longer depends on a live coroutine

- `ConnectionService` schedules a one-minute `setAndAllowWhileIdle` alarm that re-delivers its own
  intent. Every tick calls `GatewayClient.wakeReconnect()`, which restarts the retry loop if it is
  not active. A frozen process cannot advance its own `delay`, so without an external tick a
  dropped socket stayed dropped until the user opened the app
- `GatewayClient.current` is a process-wide reference so the service can drive reconnects without
  holding the Activity; after a process kill the service rebuilds a client from the stored pairing
  (`ensureClient`), and `MainActivity` reuses that instance instead of creating a second one
- Combined with the wake lock, Wi-Fi high-performance lock and the 60s gateway `ping_timeout`, the
  socket now has four independent reasons to stay up or come back

## Pairing code rendered as PNG; exact-alarm watchdog

- The panel now receives `qr_png` (base64) and renders it as an `<img>`; the SVG path draws modules
  as stroked paths that every browser rasterises slightly differently, which is exactly what a
  camera cannot resolve. The PNG is built from segno's matrix with PIL at scale 12 / border 4,
  giving 540x540 for a version 5 code
- `test-qr-from-panel.png` is the byte-identical output of that generator, so it can be compared
  against what the panel shows
- The reconnect watchdog uses `setExactAndAllowWhileIdle` when exact alarms are permitted and
  falls back to `setAndAllowWhileIdle`; the foreground notification text now mirrors the real
  socket state ("Connected to desktop" / "Reconnecting…")

## Recording why the socket dies

- `GatewayClient` keeps `lastDisconnectReason`, `lastDisconnectAt` and `reconnectAttempts`, and
  Settings shows them as a `Link:` row. Every previous round added another keep-alive mechanism
  without ever establishing whether the socket was being dropped by the gateway, by the network,
  or whether the retry loop ran at all — these three values separate those cases
- `onClosed` records the peer's close code; `onFailure` records the transport error; the reconnect
  loop counts its attempts so a frozen loop (count stuck) is distinguishable from a blocked
  network (count climbing, still failing)

## Foreground service type was the reason it kept disappearing

- The常驻 notification vanishing was the giveaway: the service itself was being stopped, and the
  frozen process then lost the socket. `dataSync` is capped at a few hours per day on Android 14+
  and is force-stopped without restart when the budget runs out
- Switched to `foregroundServiceType="connectedDevice"` with
  `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE`, which is the type intended for maintaining a link to a
  paired device and carries no daily budget. `startForeground` passes the matching type constant
- The exact-alarm watchdog, `START_STICKY` and `onTaskRemoved` restart all remain, so a service
  killed for any other reason is still brought back

## Background layer rewritten

- Previous design killed itself: `startInForeground`'s catch called `stopSelf()`, so any refusal of
  `startForeground` (OEM policy, revoked grant, unsupported type) turned a permission problem into
  "service gone, notification gone, socket dead" within seconds. The service now degrades to an
  ordinary background service and reports the reason instead of dying
- `PebbleApp` (Application) owns `GatewayClient` and starts the service on process start, so the
  connection no longer depends on an Activity ever running; `MainActivity` reuses that instance
- `ConnectionService` is now deliberately dumb: it raises priority, holds the wake and Wi-Fi locks,
  and ticks once a minute to reschedule a frozen retry loop. No connection state, no retry policy
- `goForeground()` is re-asserted on every tick because the system can demote the service without
  notice

## The socket was closed by our own code on every task switch

- `MainActivity.onDestroy()` called `client.close()`, and `PebbleRoot` had
  `DisposableEffect { onDispose { client.stopAutoReconnect() } }`. Android destroys the Activity on
  every task switch, so leaving the app actively closed the socket and cancelled the retry loop.
  Every keep-alive mechanism added in earlier rounds was working correctly and being undone by
  these two lines
- Both removed. The connection is owned by `PebbleApp` and now genuinely outlives the UI; only the
  explicit "disconnect pairing" actions close it
- Remaining lifetime rule: anything that should survive a task switch must not be tied to an
  Activity or a composition scope

## Reconnect is fast, and missed messages are reported

- Retry backoff now starts at 200ms (doubling to 30s) instead of 1s, so the one or two second gap
  the user sees is as short as it can be
- `PebbleApp` watches the connection and, on every transition into `Connected`, compares
  `session.list`'s `last_message_at` against `Prefs.lastSeenAt` (updated while online). Anything
  newer means a broadcast was produced while the phone was offline — those frames are
  fire-and-forget and cannot be replayed — so one summary notification is raised
- `software caused connection abort` is the transport reporting a locally aborted socket, which is
  what a suspended network or a Wi-Fi hand-off looks like; it cannot always be prevented, so the
  catch-up path is what makes the behaviour correct

## Two bugs that made catch-up never fire

- `Prefs.lastSeenAt` was written in milliseconds while `session.list` reports `last_message_at` in
  seconds, so `last_message_at > lastSeenAt` was false for every session and the catch-up
  notification could never trigger. Stored in seconds now, and a legacy millisecond value is
  treated as absent
- `Notifier.post` decided visibility from the Activity callbacks, which go stale once the Activity
  is destroyed — a stale "foreground" silently swallowed every notification. It now asks the system
  for this process's `IMPORTANCE_FOREGROUND`, falling back to the callback only if that fails

## Half-open sockets hid every missed message

- `broken pipe` means the peer had already closed while we kept writing: the socket is half-open,
  `ConnState` still reads `Connected`, and no event arrives
- `lastSeenAt` was refreshed on every `Connected` state emission, so during a half-open period it
  kept advancing to "now" and `last_message_at > lastSeenAt` could never be true. The marker is now
  refreshed only when a frame is actually delivered
- Catch-up has three triggers: the disconnected→connected edge, a one-minute service tick, and the
  per-frame marker. A half-open socket is caught by the tick plus the frozen marker

## Notification path isolated from the socket reader, and made observable

- `onMessage` no longer calls `Notifier.dispatch`. It parses, records, and hands
  the event to a channel; dispatch runs on a separate coroutine. OkHttp's reader
  treats any exception from a listener callback as a connection failure, so a
  throw while posting a notification could close the socket that delivered it
- `Diag` records per-stage counters: frames in, events delivered, sends ok and
  failed, drops with their source (`onFailure`/`onClosed`/`close`), connect
  attempts versus successes, live sockets, notification posts and the reason for
  each suppression, service ticks, catch-up runs. Settings renders them and
  offers a reset
- `connect()` cancels the previous socket before opening a new one; without it a
  reconnect leaked a WebSocket, and the gateway caps connections at 8
- `pingInterval` raised to 30s to match the gateway's `ping_interval=30`
- `ConnectionService` ticks every 30s and repaints its ongoing notification on
  every connection state change
- `catchUp` no longer gates on `AppVisibility`; `post()` decides visibility and
  records which rule suppressed it

## Reconnect loop is idempotent; timeline is measured in seconds

- `startAutoReconnect` returns early when the target, token and live job are unchanged.
  The Activity is recreated on every task switch and re-ran its `LaunchedEffect`,
  which cancelled a healthy socket and dropped everything sent during the
  reconnect window
- `Diag.ago` always reports a number of seconds. Collapsing the first second into
  "just now" made a drop on resume indistinguishable from one during background
- `Diag.foregroundAt` / `backgroundAt` are recorded from the activity callbacks, so
  the drop timestamp can be placed against the foreground transitions

## Half-open sockets are now detected instead of waited out

- `pingInterval` is 15s: a half-open link used to stay undetected for up to 30s,
  and the failure only surfaced when the next write happened to hit the dead socket
- `GatewayClient.dropIfStale()` runs on every service tick. A socket that reports
  `Connected` but has delivered no frame for 45s is cancelled so the reconnect loop
  starts, instead of sitting there silently losing frames
- `ConnectionState` exposes `wifiLockHeld` and `wifiLockError`; acquiring the
  high-performance Wi-Fi lock previously failed silently inside `runCatching`, and a
  missing lock is what lets Wi-Fi power save abort the connection in the background

- 2026-10-23 Android client transport and background keeping. Every socket teardown path goes through `invalidateSocket(expected, ...)`, which clears the reference, moves the state and fails pending calls as one step, and ignores a callback from a socket that has already been replaced. `connect()` bounds the opening handshake at 15 s. Forced restarts are gated: none while a handshake is inside its window, and at most one per 2 s while the loop is alive. `onFailure` restarts the loop only when the handshake had already completed. `ConnectionService` is a `connectedDevice` foreground service holding a partial wake lock and a high-performance Wi-Fi lock; a `startForeground` failure stops the service, so the platform FGS timeout has no process left to kill. `catchUpNow()` posts one notification per changed session. `gatewayReady` is `connState is ConnState.Connected`; `gateway.ready` is a one-shot frame and is not used as a latch. Settings exposes the battery-optimisation and auto-start entry points plus a test-notification button; the in-memory counter panel is removed, along with the `Diag` surface it read.

- 2026-10-23 Android conversation UI. The thinking card starts expanded and measures its own duration, from the first `thinking.delta` of a block to the first visible token, a tool start, or `message.complete`, rendered as `850ms` / `1.2s` with the character count as the fallback for replayed history. The transcript follows the tail as a mode rather than a viewport measurement: it starts engaged, the user's own drag (`DragInteraction.Start`) disengages it, and reaching the bottom (`canScrollForward` false) or pressing the jump-to-latest button engages it again; while engaged it scrolls the trailing spacer without animation, and a floating circle button appears whenever the follow is disengaged. A 2 dp accent sweep bar sits at the composer's top edge while a turn is running, replaced by the hairline divider when idle. `message.complete` clears `streaming`, so the composer returns to send mode on its own, and the app bar title comes from `session.resume`'s `info.title`, which the gateway also returns from `session.create`.
